WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
Speaker 1: Well, as many of you know, every two&nbsp;
or three months we have a last minute cancellation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:11.280
or a no show or a problem with a guest. And it's&nbsp;
unfortunate, but it is impossible to prevent 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.680
when that happens. I say let's hear from a few&nbsp;
people in the audience, so let's do that. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:23.160
of course, we take calls via discord at David&nbsp;
Pakman dot com slash discord. Let's start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.840
Mitchell from Ohio. Mitchell, what's on&nbsp;
your mind today? What can I do for you?&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:36.420
Speaker 3: Hi, David. I've been thinking&nbsp;
a lot recently about how most people on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.280
opposite sides of the political spectrum&nbsp;
have more in common with each other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:48.900
they will ever have with people who have a vast&nbsp;
amount of influence and power in this country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.600
True. And I just can't seem to wrap my head&nbsp;
around how we get people to understand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.620
their interests are more aligned than they think.
Speaker 1: I don't know the answer to that either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:06.840
And you know what? The first time that this was&nbsp;
like, very evident to me was that in a movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.760
I covered closely. It was the 2010, the growth&nbsp;
of the Tea Party movement. And Jonathan Haidt has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.820
written a lot about this, where a lot of it has&nbsp;
to do with you're not thinking in terms of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.320
is actually best for me. And the vast majority&nbsp;
of people, including people who supposedly are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.540
on the opposite political side. You're thinking&nbsp;
in terms of my team, you're thinking in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.980
of the people who I sort of take direction&nbsp;
from. What are they telling me to do? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:38.760
are they saying is best? And I genuinely don't&nbsp;
know. A lot of this has very deep psychology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.120
written into it, as Jonathan Haidt has written&nbsp;
about. And I don't because you're absolutely right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.820
on so many issues, particularly economic issues,&nbsp;
even if we put aside social issue differences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.860
the vast majority of Americans actually&nbsp;
have the same interests and would benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.960
from the same policies, and yet they&nbsp;
are split between two political parties.&nbsp;

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:08.160
Speaker 3: I'd have to agree with you. And if you&nbsp;
ask anyone from either party or either side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.560
the aisle, I feel like most people just want to be&nbsp;
able to put in an honest day's work for an honest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:19.020
day's wage, come home to their family and do it&nbsp;
again the next day and put a little money aside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.900
But do you think that there's truth to that? There&nbsp;
is an artificial culture war being started by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.240
you know, people like Ron DeSantis to try&nbsp;
and stop people from seeing the economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.160
issues that they actually have in common.
Speaker 1: That's one way to put it. The way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.540
and I don't think you're wrong the way I've&nbsp;
said it is a little different, which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:46.380
country keeps moving left on policy, particularly&nbsp;
economic policy. So what the right has done is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:52.440
abandoned policy, particularly economic policy,&nbsp;
and do the culture war. So I don't know that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:58.440
overtly to prevent people from realizing that's&nbsp;
an interesting perspective. But I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:04.980
basically if it was just about policy, Republicans&nbsp;
would have an even tougher time winning elections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.940
So when they make it about something else instead.&nbsp;
But it's basically the same thing you're saying.&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.800
Speaker 3: And it's all it's all very&nbsp;
interesting. And I appreciate your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.740
perspective. Thank you for taking my call.
Speaker 1: All right, Mitchell from Ohio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.800
very important and well-said. I appreciate that.

